A||||12||MooCow||Squeeky wheels what to do????||||8||
Z||000000||MooCow||08-21-2000||11:07 PM||||My wheels are making noises will going 50 or lower all the time.  It happens worse when I turn the steering wheel roght just a little bit.  Also everytime I hit the brakes it squeeks at first tehn stops when I press them hard.  How can I fix this problem so my car will not squeek anymore??  Are there any web links to help out??   Can I spay the rims and such with WD-40??  Please help cause this noise is really making me mad.<BR>                          Thanks Ken||||reg||8
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||08-21-2000||11:09 PM||||Do any brake work lately?<p>[This message has been edited by Indiana_resto_guy (edited 08-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Shiner||08-22-2000||12:43 AM||||I'm with Indiana Dan here: wheels don't sqeek.  Check the brakes and wheel bearings.  Jack the car up, leave the wheels on, grab them with your hands, and see how much play they have in them.  I am willing to be this is a wheel bearing or brake problem.||||reg||1
Z||000003||AL||08-22-2000||01:23 AM||||change bearings change pads,take rotors and have them cut,before you install rotors clean them with water and rag install pads,make sure you don't touch the rotors with greas hands,and that goes for the pads also.do all this and no more noise.<B>AL</B> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AL_sig.gif"> <P>------------------<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||HugGRRRFiero||08-22-2000||08:08 AM||||Don't spray your wheels with WD-40.  Use only good wheel bearing grease...  Like they say, the squeaky wheel always gets the grease.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Seriously, check out your wheel bearings and brakes like everyone is saying.<P>Brent<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">  <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||3
Z||000005||MooCow||08-22-2000||10:58 AM||||Thanks but I know for a fact the brakes are brand new almost.  When it rains the squeek will stop for a while but always comes back to get me.  I will take the wheels of and greese the beraing and see if that helps.<P>                               Thanks Ken||||reg||1
Z||000006||JohnnyK||08-22-2000||01:54 PM||||If it stops when it rains, then I would bet it would be in the brakes.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||08-22-2000||02:25 PM||||It's most likely brakes.<P>New brakes can and will make all sorts of noise, especially if the rotors weren't machined and/or cleaned properly when installed.||||reg||1
Z||000008||whamel||08-22-2000||02:37 PM||||Any chance it might be in the type of brake pad you used?  I'm not sure if that might have anything to do with it.<BR>Either that or maybe something got on them that caused them to go bad.  I had a shock absorber pop and blew oil all over my rotors.  Ate my brake pads in about a week.||||reg||1
Z||000009||HugGRRRFiero||08-22-2000||03:13 PM||||Ok, if this is a new problem since you put new brakes on then, I agree with ogre, they could cause some squealing "breaking in" to an uneven rotor surface.  <P>First, take the wheel off and check your rotors to see if they have grooves in them, if they are bad consider having them replaced.<P>The source of the squeal could also be the new pads.  The interface between the backface of the pad and caliper piston, the overall shape of the pad, and pad material, could all be causes of the vibration.  <P>I'd say that the problem isn't related to the wheel bearings because it sounds like you're dampening out the brake vibration when you press the brake pedal hard.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">  <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||AL||08-22-2000||08:00 PM||||your rotors are glazed up, have them cut. <B>AL</B> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AL_sig.gif"> <P>------------------<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||crazygt||08-22-2000||08:21 PM||||this sounds like you need to lube up your front upper and lower  ball joints and tie rod ends. When you lube them up make sure you turn the steering wheel lock to lock a few times also check for play in the front end aswell . ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Taijiguy||08-23-2000||10:21 PM||||Just to throw my hypothesis out here, it is your brakes/pads. New or old, they are subject to squealing, although It often depends on the pad. I always suggest bonded pads over riveted, comonly riveted pads will squeak the worst. Also, during assembly, you can apply a high temperature silicone to the surface of the pad, where it contacts the piston, on the inside pad, or the caliper on the outside pad. (But of course, NOT on the friction surface) This will also help quiet squeaking. <P>-Taiji||||reg||1
